WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.940
Why do similar-looking hindbrain segments make different neurons?

2
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.520
Each segment carries an address written in Hox transcription factors.

3
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.860
Its mix of Hox proteins, working with segment genes such as Krox20, helps set which

4
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.220
neurons it makes and where their axons go.

5
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.980
That matters for jaw and face movement.

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.680
The small bulges are called rhombomeres.

7
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.780
In chick, r2 and r3 make trigeminal motor neurons; r4 and r5 make facial motor

8
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:33.780
neurons.

9
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.830
Test that address by removing Hoxb1 in mice.

10
00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.250
Normally, facial motor neurons are born in r4 and migrate back to r6.

11
00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.390
Without Hoxb1, r4 loses parts of its identity.

12
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.950
Its motor neurons fail to form or migrate normally; some behave like trigeminal

13
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.870
neurons. The facial motor nucleus does not form normally.

14
00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.030
The segment is still there.

15
00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.750
What changes is what its cells become.

16
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.210
The addresses also sit in compartments.

17
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.370
In chick, descendants of a cell labelled before boundaries appear can span

18
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.490
neighbouring rhombomeres.

19
00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:15.330
Label a cell after boundaries appear, and its descendants stay within one.

20
00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:17.950
Cell sorting helps keep borders sharp.

21
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:24.070
In fish and frog, EphA4 receptors and ephrin-B ligands on neighbouring cells promote

22
00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:25.210
sorting at contact.

23
00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.170
These borders restrict progenitor mixing.

24
00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.750
Later neurons can still migrate, as the facial motor neurons do.

25
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.580
What helps establish the Hox code?

26
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:42.640
Retinoic acid, a product of vitamin A. Raldh2 makes it in mesoderm beside the future

27
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.300
hindbrain and spinal cord.

28
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.660
Cyp26 enzymes destroy it, with Cyp26a1 strongly expressed toward the front.

29
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.540
Like a bath with a tap and drain, supply behind and destruction in front leave more

30
00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.440
signal at the back and less at the front.

31
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:02.793
Retinoic acid reaches the nucleus and binds RAR.

32
00:02:03.753 --> 00:02:09.573
This receptor works with RXR on short DNA sequences called retinoic acid response

33
00:02:09.573 --> 00:02:13.613
elements. Hoxa1 and Hoxb1 carry these elements.

34
00:02:14.613 --> 00:02:20.233
Retinoic acid helps switch on the front-most Hox genes, working alongside FGF and

35
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:21.233
Wnt signals.

36
00:02:22.283 --> 00:02:24.483
Change this input and the code moves.

37
00:02:25.363 --> 00:02:30.303
In mouse embryos exposed to extra retinoic acid early, Hox domains changed.

38
00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:34.883
Rhombomeres 2 and 3 shifted toward the identity of 4 and 5.

39
00:02:35.703 --> 00:02:37.723
The signal helps set regional identity.

40
00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:40.867
The chromosome carries an order too.

41
00:02:41.887 --> 00:02:45.487
Hox genes at the 3′ end reach farthest forward along the body.

42
00:02:46.447 --> 00:02:49.607
Genes toward the 5′ end have front edges farther back.

43
00:02:50.447 --> 00:02:55.467
In mouse and chick, 3′ genes also switch on first, and 5′ genes later.

44
00:02:56.327 --> 00:03:01.227
This is colinearity: chromosome order matches position and timing along the growing

45
00:03:01.227 --> 00:03:01.547
body.
